{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,200p' docs/deployment/stripe-setup.md\"","aggregated_output":"/opt/homebrew/Library/Homebrew/cmd/shellenv.sh: line 18: /bin/ps: Operation not permitted\n# Stripe Setup Guide\n\nThis guide provides step-by-step instructions for configuring Stripe for the HeroKid application, covering account creation, API key management, webhook setup, product configuration, and local testing.\n\n## 1. Create a Stripe Account\n\nIf you don't already have one, create a Stripe account:\n1. Go to [Stripe's website](https://stripe.com/).\n2. Click \"Start now\" or \"Sign up\".\n3. Follow the on-screen instructions to create your account.\n\n## 2. Get API Keys\n\nStripe uses two types of API keys: publishable keys (for client-side code) and secret keys (for server-side code). You'll need both for your test and live environments.\n\n1.  **Log in** to your Stripe Dashboard.\n2.  Navigate to **Developers > API keys**.\n3.  You will see your **Publishable key** (starts with `pk_test_` or `pk_live_`) and **Secret key** (starts with `sk_test_` or `sk_live_`).\n    *   For development and testing, use the **test keys**.\n    *   For production, switch to **live keys**.\n\n## 3. Set up Webhook Endpoint for `/api/webhooks/stripe`\n\nWebhooks are crucial for Stripe to communicate events back to your application (e.g., successful payments, subscription changes).\n\n1.  In your Stripe Dashboard, navigate to **Developers > Webhooks**.\n2.  Click **\"Add endpoint\"**.\n3.  **Endpoint URL**:\n    *   For local development: Use the Stripe CLI (see Section 6) or a tunneling service like ngrok to expose your local `http://localhost:3000/api/webhooks/stripe` endpoint to a public URL. Enter that public URL here.\n    *   For production: Enter your live application's webhook URL, e.g., `https://yourdomain.com/api/webhooks/stripe`.\n4.  **Events to send**: Select the following events (or as required by the application):\n    *   `checkout.session.completed`\n    *   `invoice.payment_succeeded`\n    *   `customer.subscription.deleted`\n    *   `payment_intent.succeeded`\n5.  Click **\"Add endpoint\"**.\n\n## 4. Configure Credit Pack Products\n\nYou need to create products in Stripe representing the credit packs users can purchase.\n\n1.  In your Stripe Dashboard, navigate to **Products > Product catalog**.\n2.  Click **\"Add product\"**.\n\n    **Product 1: 1 Credit Pack**\n    *   **Name**: `HeroKid 1 Credit Pack`\n    *   **Description**: `Purchase 1 credit for HeroKid`\n    *   **Pricing model**: `Standard pricing`\n    *   **Price**: `0.99` (USD)\n    *   **Billing period**: `One-time`\n    *   **Save product**.\n\n    **Product 2: 5 Credits Pack**\n    *   **Name**: `HeroKid 5 Credit Pack`\n    *   **Description**: `Purchase 5 credits for HeroKid`\n    *   **Pricing model**: `Standard pricing`\n    *   **Price**: `4.49` (USD)\n    *   **Billing period**: `One-time`\n    *   **Save product**.\n\n    **Product 3: 10 Credits Pack**\n    *   **Name**: `HeroKid 10 Credit Pack`\n    *   **Description**: `Purchase 10 credits for HeroKid`\n    *   **Pricing model**: `Standard pricing`\n    *   **Price**: `7.99` (USD)\n    *   **Billing period**: `One-time`\n    *   **Save product**.\n\n3.  After creating each product, note down its **Price ID** (starts with `price_`). You will need these for your environment variables.\n\n## 5. Get Webhook Signing Secret\n\nThe webhook signing secret is used to verify that incoming webhooks are actually from Stripe and not from a malicious third party.\n\n1.  In your Stripe Dashboard, navigate to **Developers > Webhooks**.\n2.  Find the webhook endpoint you created in Section 3.\n3.  Click on the endpoint.\n4.  Under \"Signing secret\", click **\"Reveal\"**.\n5.  Copy the secret (starts with `whsec_`). This will be used as `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` in your environment variables.\n\n## 6. Testing Webhook Locally with Stripe CLI\n\nThe Stripe CLI is an invaluable tool for local development and testing webhooks.\n\n1.  **Install the Stripe CLI**: Follow the instructions on the [Stripe CLI documentation](https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-cli).\n    *   For macOS, you can use Homebrew: `brew install stripe/stripe-cli/stripe`\n2.  **Login to the Stripe CLI**:\n    ```bash\n    stripe login\n    ```\n    This will open a browser window for authentication.\n3.  **Forward webhooks to your local server**:\n    ```bash\n    stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/webhooks/stripe\n    ```\n    This command will provide a webhook secret (different from your dashboard secret) and start forwarding events. You'll use the secret provided by this command as your `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` when running locally.\n4.  **Trigger test events**: You can trigger test events directly from the Stripe CLI (e.g., `stripe trigger checkout.session.completed`) or by performing test purchases through your application in test mode.\n    *   Observe your local application's console output to ensure the webhook is received and processed correctly.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
